WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:04.260
What if Natalie Portman battled the&nbsp;
undead in Pride and Prejudice and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.260 --> 00:00:08.400
Zombies? And would John Krasinski have&nbsp;
won best actor for "Manchester By The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:11.506
Sea?" These are Hollywood's biggest "what if's."

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:17.280
"Gone Girl" is a 2014 psychological thriller&nbsp;
that tells the story of Nick Dunne and his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:21.720
missing wife Amy, who disappears on the day&nbsp;
of the fifth wedding anniversary. The prime&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:26.400
suspect in the case, Nick must try to clear&nbsp;
his name while uncovering the truth about what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:31.200
has happened to Amy. The film is based&nbsp;
on Gillian Flynn's 2012 novel and was a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:38.160
financial and critical hit, earning $369 million&nbsp;
worldwide against a reported $61 million budget.

00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:44.460
According to The Washington Post, Reese&nbsp;
Witherspoon played a major part in developing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.460 --> 00:00:49.560
the film and was initially set to star as Amy.&nbsp;
However, the role eventually went to Rosamund&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:54.060
Pike, who is not much younger than Witherspoon.&nbsp;
Speaking in a roundtable event hosted by The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:58.260
Hollywood Reporter, Witherspoon revealed that&nbsp;
after talking with director David Fincher,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.260 --> 00:01:02.220
she came to the conclusion that she was not&nbsp;
the right person to portray Amy on screen.

00:01:02.220 --> 00:01:06.180
Whatever conversation Witherspoon&nbsp;
and Fincher had seemed to have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:09.960
for the best. Not only was Pike widely&nbsp;
praised for her performance in the movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:13.620
but she was also nominated for the&nbsp;
Academy Award for Best Actress.

00:01:13.967 --> 00:01:20.520
"You think I'd let him destroy me and&nbsp;
end up happier than ever? No f—ing way."

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:26.340
Directed by Martin Scorsese's, the crime thriller&nbsp;
"The Departed" was actually a remake of the 2002&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.340 --> 00:01:32.160
film "Infernal Affairs." The story follows two&nbsp;
undercover agents, one working for the mob and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:37.200
one for the police, as they try to infiltrate&nbsp;
each organization and uncover the identity of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:42.240
the opposing mole. The film stars Leonardo&nbsp;
DiCaprio as state trooper William Costigan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:47.940
Matt Damon as the mob spy Colin Sullivan, and&nbsp;
Jack Nicholson as mob boss Frank Costello.

00:01:47.940 --> 00:01:52.440
Brad Pitt and his production company Plan&nbsp;
B purchased the rights to remake "Internal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:57.600
Affairs," beating out disgraced film producer&nbsp;
Harvey Weinstein, via Variety. The actor and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.600 --> 00:02:02.520
producer told Interview magazine that he worked&nbsp;
on the project for more than two years to try&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:07.260
and get it made before securing the services&nbsp;
of Scorsese and scriptwriter William Monahan.

00:02:07.260 --> 00:02:10.860
Early reports suggested that Pitt&nbsp;
would take on the part of Sullivan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:13.800
in the movie. However the actor&nbsp;
stepped back from the role when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:17.280
Scorsese signed on to direct. In&nbsp;
the same interview, Pitt explained:

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:21.300
"I thought it would be better if they were&nbsp;
younger guys that were just starting their lives,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:26.298
guys coming out of the academy, guys who&nbsp;
were hungry. I thought I was too old for it."

00:02:26.298 --> 00:02:30.540
Although "The Batman" was originally meant&nbsp;
to be part of the DC Extended Universe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.540 --> 00:02:34.980
the Matt Reeves-directed movie is now&nbsp;
considered a standalone project separate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.980 --> 00:02:39.480
from the larger continuity. This is largely&nbsp;
down to the insistence of the director, who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:44.220
wanted to tell an earlier story about the Caped&nbsp;
Crusader and his struggles to battle crime in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.220 --> 00:02:49.140
Gotham. This version of the movie was co-written&nbsp;
with Peter Craig and stars Robert Pattinson.

00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:51.314
"I'm vengeance."

00:02:51.314 --> 00:02:56.640
"The Batman" had a troubled production, like&nbsp;
many DC movie projects, and it wasn't clear when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.640 --> 00:03:01.740
it would actually get made. Ben Affleck, who had&nbsp;
already portrayed Batman in three earlier films,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.740 --> 00:03:07.620
was set to direct, write, and star in the&nbsp;
movie. But by 2017, Variety was reporting that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:12.120
Affleck had chosen to step down from directing&nbsp;
duties to focus on producing and performing.

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:17.160
The actor later chose to exit the project&nbsp;
altogether. Explaining his decision in 2019,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:21.120
Affleck said that he couldn't get the&nbsp;
film to work and felt that it would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:25.320
better if someone else filled the role and&nbsp;
provided a fresh take on the character. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.320 --> 00:03:29.940
also revealed to The New York Times in 2020&nbsp;
that he had stepped back from "The Batman" due&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.940 --> 00:03:34.080
to a combination of personal issues. This&nbsp;
includes his alcoholism and divorce from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:38.550
Jennifer Garner, as well as his negative&nbsp;
experience working on "Justice League."

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:43.440
Based on Marie Brenner's 1997 Vanity&nbsp;
Fair article "American Nightmare:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:48.720
The Ballad of Richard Jewell," this movie focuses&nbsp;
on the real-life story of security guard Richard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:53.700
Jewell. He discovered the explosives at the&nbsp;
Centennial Olympic Park bombing and subsequently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.700 --> 00:03:58.980
became a suspect in the investigation. Directed&nbsp;
by Clint Eastwood, it features Paul Walter Hauser&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.980 --> 00:04:04.980
as Jewell and Sam Rockwell as Attorney General&nbsp;
Watson Bryant Jr., with Kathy Bates, Jon Hamm,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.980 --> 00:04:10.560
and Olivia Wilde all having major roles. While it&nbsp;
was not a financial success, failing to make back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:16.271
its $45 million budget, according to Box Office&nbsp;
Mojo, the film received largely positive reviews.

00:04:16.271 --> 00:04:19.920
"Please clear my son's name."

00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:25.020
Jonah Hill and Leonardo DiCaprio were&nbsp;
both attached to the film early on and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.020 --> 00:04:30.720
were expected to both produce and star. Deadline&nbsp;
reported in 2014 that Hill would play Jewell,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:35.040
while DiCaprio would take on the part of&nbsp;
Jewell's lawyer. Updates were few after that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:40.080
until Clint Eastwood was eventually signed&nbsp;
to direct. It was confirmed in 2019 that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:44.460
the two actors would no longer appear in the&nbsp;
film, with Eastwood instead looking to put&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.460 --> 00:04:49.868
together his own cast, via Deadline. However,&nbsp;
Hill and DiCaprio stayed on as producers.

00:04:50.100 --> 00:04:54.720
For many people, "Murder Mystery" is likely&nbsp;
just another film in a long line of Adam&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.720 --> 00:04:59.760
Sandler comedies, but it has proved successful&nbsp;
enough for Netflix to greenlight a sequel that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:04.680
has already begun filming. The first movie sees&nbsp;
police officer Nick Spitz and his wife Audrey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.680 --> 00:05:09.120
played by Sandler and Jennifer Aniston, as&nbsp;
they turn into murder investigators after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:12.626
witnessing a billionaire killed&nbsp;
on his own yacht during a party.

00:05:12.626 --> 00:05:14.820
"Has anybody tried to revive him?"

00:05:14.820 --> 00:05:17.903
"He's got a knife plunged in his&nbsp;
heart. I don't think he's revivable."

00:05:17.903 --> 00:05:22.560
Both director John Madden and actor Charlize&nbsp;
Theron were attached to "Murder Mystery" when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.560 --> 00:05:27.000
it was first developed. The pair were first linked&nbsp;
to the project by The Hollywood Reporter back in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:32.700
2012. THR later revealed that Madden and Theron&nbsp;
had dropped out of the project, although there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.700 --> 00:05:37.500
was no reason given why. Theron was eventually&nbsp;
credited as an executive producer, with her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.500 --> 00:05:43.020
production company Denver and Delilah Productions&nbsp;
also involved in a minor way. By the time filming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.020 --> 00:05:49.168
started in 2018, Kyle Newacheck was at the&nbsp;
helm, with Sandler and Aniston as the leads.

00:05:49.260 --> 00:05:53.340
"Pride and Prejudice and Zombies" is&nbsp;
a comedy horror film that follows the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.340 --> 00:05:57.840
basic story of Jane Austen's novel "Pride&nbsp;
and Prejudice," but mashes it together with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.840 --> 00:06:02.280
modern zombie elements. The end result is&nbsp;
a movie where the Bennet sisters search for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.280 --> 00:06:06.300
suitable suitors while also defending&nbsp;
themselves from hordes of the undead.

00:06:06.300 --> 00:06:09.420
Burr Steers led the project,&nbsp;
writing and directing the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.420 --> 00:06:15.000
while Lily James and Sam Riley starred. However,&nbsp;
James was not the first person considered for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:20.700
the lead role of Elizabeth Bennet. In 2009,&nbsp;
Variety reported that Natalie Portman would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.700 --> 00:06:25.260
producing and starring in the film through her&nbsp;
own production company, Handsomecharlie Films.

00:06:25.260 --> 00:06:29.160
Her departure from "Pride and Prejudice&nbsp;
and Zombies" came two days after David&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.160 --> 00:06:33.240
O. Russell also left the project.&nbsp;
As per The Wall Street Journal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.240 --> 00:06:38.100
Russell seemingly quit following a dispute with&nbsp;
Lionsgate about the film's proposed budget.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.100 --> 00:06:42.540
Although no reason was given for why Portman&nbsp;
would no longer appear, New York magazine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.540 --> 00:06:47.769
suggested it may have been down to scheduling&nbsp;
conflicts. She continued to serve as a producer.

00:06:47.880 --> 00:06:53.700
"Yes Man" is a 2009 romantic comedy starring&nbsp;
Jim Carrey as Carl, a man who attempts to turn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.700 --> 00:06:59.040
his life around by only saying yes to any and&nbsp;
all opportunities. The plot is partly based&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.040 --> 00:07:04.320
on the "Yes Man" book written by Danny Wallace,&nbsp;
who lived his life saying "yes" for six months.

00:07:04.320 --> 00:07:09.120
Before Wallace's memoir was even published,&nbsp;
Warner Bros. purchased the rights to make a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.120 --> 00:07:14.460
film. According to The Advocate, Jack Black and&nbsp;
his "School of Rock" collaborator Mike White were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.460 --> 00:07:18.660
set to develop the movie with producer David&nbsp;
Heyman, who stayed with the project. There's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.660 --> 00:07:23.400
been no definite reason given for why Black and&nbsp;
White left "Yes Man," but Vulture suggested that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.400 --> 00:07:28.020
it was likely down to the actor being involved&nbsp;
in other films and unable to make room in his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.020 --> 00:07:32.340
schedule. This does make sense, considering&nbsp;
that the actor was involved in the high-profile&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.340 --> 00:07:37.800
movies "Be Kind Rewind," "Kung Fu Panda," and&nbsp;
"Tropic Thunder" all around the same time.

00:07:38.535 --> 00:07:40.234
"No!"

00:07:40.357 --> 00:07:40.996
"No."

00:07:44.100 --> 00:07:50.460
In the 2019 drama "Lucy in the Sky," space&nbsp;
explorer Lucy Cola, played by Natalie Portman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.460 --> 00:07:55.380
returns to Earth following a space mission but&nbsp;
finds her life deteriorating as she struggles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.380 --> 00:08:00.060
to find meaning. This eventually culminates&nbsp;
in an attack on two fellow astronauts as she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.060 --> 00:08:04.140
suffers from hallucinations and paranoia.&nbsp;
Directed and written by Noah Hawley,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.140 --> 00:08:08.700
who had previously worked on television&nbsp;
series such as "Fargo" and "Legion," it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.700 --> 00:08:14.160
was a huge financial failure, grossing just&nbsp;
over $300,000, according to Box Office Mojo.

00:08:14.160 --> 00:08:20.820
Variety confirmed in 2017 that Fox Searchlight had&nbsp;
partnered with Reese Witherspoon, Bruna Papandrea,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.820 --> 00:08:25.200
and Noah Hawley to produce the film, which&nbsp;
at that point was titled "Pale Blue Dot."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.200 --> 00:08:29.340
Witherspoon was expected to star as Lucy&nbsp;
and was attached as a producer through her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.340 --> 00:08:33.660
company Pacific Standard. Later that year,&nbsp;
Witherspoon announced that she was stepping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.660 --> 00:08:37.560
down from the cast but would remain as&nbsp;
a producer, with Deadline speculating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.560 --> 00:08:41.948
that this was probably so that she could&nbsp;
film a second season of "Big Little Lies."

00:08:42.240 --> 00:08:44.760
Following the success of&nbsp;
its other original movies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.760 --> 00:08:49.620
Apple has recently been working on a film&nbsp;
titled "Project Artemis," based on NASA's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.620 --> 00:08:53.700
Artemis program to get humans back on the&nbsp;
Moon for the first time in more than 50 years.

00:08:53.700 --> 00:08:58.800
When the film was first announced, Scarlett&nbsp;
Johansson, Chris Evans, and Jason Bateman were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.800 --> 00:09:03.300
all involved. Evans and Johansson were expected&nbsp;
to star in the movie, working together for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.300 --> 00:09:08.580
first time since they appeared in "Avengers:&nbsp;
Endgame" in 2019. Despite the fact that the two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.580 --> 00:09:13.380
had expressed a desire to team up again, it was&nbsp;
revealed just a few months later that Evans had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.380 --> 00:09:17.700
been forced to quit the role due to scheduling&nbsp;
conflicts. The Apple release would have caused&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.700 --> 00:09:22.806
issues for filming on two of his other upcoming&nbsp;
movies, "Pain Hustlers" and "Big Red One."

00:09:23.220 --> 00:09:28.080
An emotional and deeply personal story,&nbsp;
"Manchester by the Sea" focuses on the life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.080 --> 00:09:32.820
of a man who is stricken with guilt and grief&nbsp;
following the death of his three children. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.820 --> 00:09:36.720
is something that he was unintentionally&nbsp;
responsible for by accidentally starting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.720 --> 00:09:40.860
the house fire that killed them. Shocked to&nbsp;
find that he has been named his nephew's legal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.860 --> 00:09:45.240
guardian following the death of his brother, he&nbsp;
is forced to confront his past and deal with his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.240 --> 00:09:50.940
turmoil. The film was lauded by critics, with&nbsp;
a 96% approval rating on Rotten Tomatoes and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.940 --> 00:09:55.260
particular praise for star Casey Affleck,&nbsp;
who won an Academy Award for Best Actor.

00:09:55.260 --> 00:09:59.940
Matt Damon was heavily involved in "Manchester&nbsp;
by the Sea" from its conception. According to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.940 --> 00:10:04.860
Rolling Stone, Damon developed the basic idea&nbsp;
for the story with actor John Krasinski . The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.860 --> 00:10:09.720
pair later approached writer-director Kenneth&nbsp;
Lonergan to further expand the plot and come up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.720 --> 00:10:14.760
with a screenplay. Explaining the creative process&nbsp;
in an interview with Variety, Lonergan said that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.760 --> 00:10:19.500
Damon was set to direct the film while Krasinski&nbsp;
would have starred as the leading character. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.500 --> 00:10:23.940
two actors ended up being unavailable and had to&nbsp;
drop out as they were busy with other projects,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.940 --> 00:10:29.087
leaving the space open for Casey Affleck to take&nbsp;
over in the main role and Lonergan to direct.

00:10:29.087 --> 00:10:31.238
"We don't -- We don't exactly&nbsp;
know what we're doing."

00:10:31.238 --> 00:10:35.340
"25th Hour," a film written by "Game of&nbsp;
Thrones" showrunner David Benioff and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.340 --> 00:10:39.300
based on his novel of the same name,&nbsp;
tells the story of Monty Brogan as he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.300 --> 00:10:43.440
enjoys one final day of freedom. About to&nbsp;
go to prison over a drug deal conviction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.440 --> 00:10:48.720
and having been sentenced to seven years behind&nbsp;
bars, he explores New York and spends time with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.720 --> 00:10:53.100
some of his family and friends. He recalls&nbsp;
the events that led him to this moment and his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.100 --> 00:10:58.020
potential future. Spike Lee took on directing&nbsp;
duties while Edward Norton starred as Monty.

00:10:58.020 --> 00:11:03.300
Speaking to The Guardian in 2003, Benioff&nbsp;
revealed how Tobey Maguire had expressed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.300 --> 00:11:07.200
an interest in producing and starring in&nbsp;
a film adaptation of the novel. The actor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.200 --> 00:11:10.500
had read a preliminary copy some&nbsp;
six months before it was due to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.500 --> 00:11:15.000
published and acquired the rights. Maguire&nbsp;
requested that Benioff write the screenplay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.000 --> 00:11:19.020
but was ultimately forced to back out of&nbsp;
starring in the film due to his commitments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.020 --> 00:11:23.820
with "Spider-Man." However, like many actors&nbsp;
on this list, he remained as a producer.

